I(51,[64],'conjecture',[61],'2205–2241','un groupe pro-réductif','Assuming a rigid semisimple Q_p tensor category and an exact faithful Q_p fibre functor T_p, its tensor automorphism group is proreductive.','The manuscript assumes the fibre functor; it does not construct it. Arbitrary filtered F-isocrystals require admissibility, and an effective F-stable lattice condition is not closed under duals.','agrees','The categorical conclusion is conditional; the intended arithmetic category is not completely specified.')
I(52,[66],'unproved claim',[63],'2259–2303','il suffit que T_p soit pleinement fidèle','The page proposes full faithfulness of T_p (or Δ) as enough to identify G with the algebraic envelope of the Galois image, then uses two gradings and an isomorphism torsor.','Full faithfulness alone does not force the image closed under subobjects; restriction Rep(SL₂)→Rep(B) for a Borel is an exact fully faithful tensor functor with a proper algebraic image. A subobject condition or suitable semisimplicity in the target is needed.','understates','The earlier note gives the correct modern caveat, but the explicit categorical counterexample is supplied here. It refutes the formal implication, not an unspecified actual arithmetic functor.')
I(53,[68],'computation',[65],'2329–2370','ρ∈C(−1)','A choice of Tate-period generator trivializes a comparison torsor; rescaling should act by a Hodge cocharacter and Galois action gives a cocycle.','The printed rescaling equation omits its torsor point: f_{uρ}=f_ρ i₁(u^{-1}) under the chosen convention. In rank one u_g=g(a)/(aχ(g)) satisfies u_{gh}=u_g g(u_h).','agrees','The notes distinguish a group element from a torsor point; the missing factor is not silently inserted into the transcription.')
I(54,[70,72],'construction said to work',[67,69],'2392–2441;2462–2467','recollement; unipotent','Filtered fibre functors with a chosen graded comparison give a torsor under a unipotent subgroup of the tensor automorphism group; crystalline Frobenius is a σ-semilinear isomorphism.','After choosing a fibre-functor trivialization, the Frobenius element changes by g^{-1}bσ(g). A ramified de Rham coefficient field has no unspecified canonical lift of residue Frobenius.','agrees','The source alternates DR/cr subscripts; the distinction is substantive.')
I(55,[74,76],'conjecture',[71,73],'2494–2585;2618–2684','F_∞; f_p — —','The proposed system has Betti, de Rham, ℓ-adic and crystalline realizations, comparison torsors, local Frobenii and a real involution.','These are compatibility desiderata. The crystalline comparison map is left blank on73; a modern period-ring comparison is a separate theorem. Over W(F_p)=Z_p the base Frobenius is the identity, unlike a general residue field.','agrees','The drawings and “non?” remain exploratory; no universal existence theorem is present.')
I(56,[78],'open on the pages',[75],'2703–2722','Hodge simple, générale; Tate simple, générale','The agenda lists Hodge and Tate conjectures, in simple and general forms, without specifying all cycle and field quantifiers.','We distinguish the classical rational Hodge conjecture, Grothendieck’s corrected generalized Hodge conjecture, and Tate cycle conjectures over finitely generated fields. These are reconstructions of names, not full handwritten statements.','agrees','The earlier note properly treats this as an agenda rather than a proved theorem.')
I(57,[78],'open on the pages',[75],'2703–2722','Frobenius; connexion; double réseau ?','The remaining agenda asks for compatibility among Frobenius, de Rham connection and filtration, mentions a double lattice with “revoir”, and a Tate group over various bases.','No exact double-lattice or global Tate-group functor is specified. An admissible filtration need not be Frobenius-stable. Modern integral comparison objects give related constructions, not an identification of the two lattices on48–50.','agrees','The note records uncertainty instead of importing a modern framework as the intended object.')
I(58,[65,67,69,71,73,77],'other',[62,64,66,68,70,74],'2242–2258;2304–2328;2371–2391;2442–2461;2468–2493;2685–2702','classe fondamentale; pureté','Reused étale text: smooth base change, divisor Kummer class, purity for smooth pairs, transitivity and independence of the class from choices.','Coefficients must be invertible on the base; the canonical purity formula carries a Tate twist. The SGA4 XIX general-regular argument originally assumes resolution, unlike the smooth-pair statement.','agrees','Both continuation chains were read:70→68 and74→66→64→62. The last interpolation proof is not complete in the attached fragment.')
