# These are human-authored mathematical judgments, not generated from text cues.
C(13,[14],'φε=F_S; FV=VF=p','Relative Frobenius factors through the displayed site morphisms; on the characteristic-p reduction the two F,V composites vanish.','construction sketched','B','The precise crystal/F,V formalism is established in crystalline Dieudonné theory; deJong1995 §§2.1–2.4 supplies the definitions. The page’s site/base and all twists must be retained, so this does not validate every unlabeled shaft.',['deJong1995','Oda1969'],comp='FV=p becomes0 in characteristic p; root_math.json: BT1_and_frobenius.')
C(14,[15],'complexe parfait; H⁰,H¹','The proposed functor assigns a two-term perfect object with co-Lie/Dieudonné triangles to every finite flat p-group over the stated base.','stated without proof','C','deJong1993 Introduction and §3 constructs a relative functor from a Z/p² lift and warns against an arbitrary-base naive vector-bundle functor. Illusie1985 §2 supplies co-Lie complexes. An exact identification with every Δ and compatibility on these pages was not found; this is a residual formulation/comparison problem.',['deJong1993','Illusie1985'],comp='A two-term object has an Ext² attaching class; equal H⁰,H¹ alone cannot identify it. See proof audit §3.',confidence='medium')
C(15,[16],'On lui associe (conjecturalement)','Canceled variant of the derived crystalline construction.','corrected or withdrawn by himself on the same page, lines405–441','C','Later co-Lie and crystalline constructions provide related precise functors, but cannot turn the canceled block into a surviving theorem. The fate is withdrawal of this presentation, with modern relatives as in deJong1993 and Illusie1985.',['deJong1993','Illusie1985'],confidence='medium')
C(16,[17],'ε*(πII)i₀ ≠ Vℓ[−1]','Compatibility of the two triangles with α,β and the co-Lie F,V maps.','self-flagged; proof sketched; inconsistent equality/inequality in the supplied text','F','The opposite relations at479 and502–504 prevent identification of one exact claim. Illusie1985 §§2–3 gives the modern deformation complexes, not a license to choose the ink.',['Illusie1985'],confidence='low')
C(17,[18],'∂I∂II nul ?; ∂II∂I nul ?','The two cross-triangle degree-two composites are zero.','open on the pages','C','Formal triangulated-category axioms only kill consecutive arrows of a single triangle. A specific compatibility/Yoneda-product computation remains to be specified. The searched crystalline and deformation sources do not identify these exact two unnamed composites.',['Illusie1985','deJong1993'],comp='Degree audit: both land two degrees higher; being built from different triangles supplies no automatic zero.',confidence='medium')
C(18,[19],'relèvement; filtration','A group lift over a PD thickening is controlled by the proposed Hodge filtration.','construction sketched; broader classification open','C','For BT groups, Messing1972 V.1.6 gives the lifting equivalence under the required locally nilpotent PD and local liftability hypotheses; Illusie1985 §4 treats truncated deformation theory. The assertion for arbitrary finite flat groups with only a PD ideal remains broader.',['Messing1972','Illusie1985'],confidence='medium')
C(19,[20],'fidèle; pleinement fidèle','Faithfulness/full faithfulness of the enriched finite-group functor and its forgetful versions.','open on the pages','C','deJong–Messing1999 Prop1.2 proves faithfulness for finite flat p-groups over locally noetherian complete-intersection bases; Th4.6 gives full faithfulness for BT groups over excellent l.c.i. bases. These are distinct assertions. They do not settle the incompletely specified enriched finite-group target.',['deJongMessing1999'],confidence='medium')
C(20,[20],'F=0','Classify finite flat height-one groups by co-Lie data with Frobenius/Verschiebung operation.','asked without proof','B','deJong1993 §2 identifies the F=0 and V=0 subcategories over an arbitrary characteristic-p base. The exact equivalences are already singled out in the opened Introduction; they are a positive special case, not a classification of all the enriched objects on16–18.',['deJong1993'])
C(21,[20],'catégorie; recollement','The proposed enriched category is a stack and reconstructs all BT objects over broad bases.','open on the pages','C','A precise modern replacement exists: Lau’s divided Dieudonné crystals classify BT groups for F-finite F-nilpotent characteristic-p schemes (Th1.3, first circulation2018, publication2026). Anschütz–LeBras classify BT groups over quasisyntomic rings using admissible prismatic crystals. Neither identifies the manuscript’s unfinished category verbatim.',['Lau2018_2026','AnschutzLeBras2023','AnschutzLeBras2025Erratum'],confidence='medium')
C(22,[21],'ker F=im V; n=ω','BT1 exactness is used to recover the Hodge submodule from a Frobenius-pullback equality.','proof sketched with an inserted hypothesis','C','The exactness is a BT1 condition, not a condition on all groups killed by p. Oda1969 Cor5.11 recovers the abelian Hodge subspace over a perfect field. The manuscript’s deduction over a nonreduced base still needs descent and the specified crystal structure.',['Oda1969','Illusie1985'],comp='root_math.json: alpha_p violates kerF=imV; multiplication by eps becomes0 under Frobenius pullback.')
C(23,[22],'rel Ext⁰=0; obstruction Ext²','Relative Ext² controls obstructions, Ext¹ choices and Ext⁰ automorphisms in the proposed deformation problem; Ext⁰ is asserted to vanish.','self-flagged framework; last obstruction marked sans doute','F','Illusie1985 Prop3.2 gives an actual cotangent-complex obstruction group. Here the relative RHom map and degrees are not fixed, so the asserted Ext⁰ vanishing is not an identifiable theorem. A homotopy fibre must be defined first.',['Illusie1985'],confidence='low')
C(24,[23],'ker ≠0','The final filtered-complex kernel supplies a counterexample to a forgetful functor.','stated without a realized example','C','The linear kernel strategy is intelligible, but the page does not construct a finite flat group realizing the data. Modern Dieudonné classifications put conditions on the target and therefore cannot by themselves validate the suggested example.',['deJong1993','Lau2018_2026'],confidence='medium')
C(25,[24],'Pseudo-rational double points','The typed quadratic-transform steps for double points are valid under Lipman’s hypotheses.','reused proof fragment','E','Identified with Lipman1969 §24, especially pp256–257. The printed page predates or is independent of the later handwriting. Our one-chart computation verifies only the A1 model and does not stand in for the classification.',['Lipman1969'],comp='root_math.json: z²−xy, x-chart strict transform w²−v has derivative−1 in v.')
C(26,[25],'réduction projective; effacement','Proper base change for torsion étale coefficients, the effacement reductions and projective reduction.','reused draft with canceled proof fragments and one deleted assertion','A','Published in SGA4 XII Th5.1, Lem6.2, Prop6.8 and Lem8.1 (Artin’s exposition). The manuscript is a draft of that material, not a separate proof. The deleted assertion(i) and index-confused canceled vanishing are not retained.',['SGA4XII'],confidence='high')
C(27,[26],'H¹(Q mod A,μ₂) ≃ (∏Z/2)/±1','A relative Kummer group is a product of local signs modulo the diagonal, with trivial relative automorphisms.','withdrawn on the same page','F','The global-to-local mapping-fibre reconstruction gives these conclusions only after defining the arithmetic base and product convention. The page’s A is not identified; standard Kummer theory does not settle its exact notation.',['StacksKummer'],comp='root_math.json: rational-square local injectivity and diagonal μ₂ injectivity; proof audit §5.',confidence='low')
C(28,[28],'||f(z)||≤M|z|^k','Banach-valued Schwarz bound and equicontinuity from the polydisc Cauchy derivative bound2M/ρ.','proved in the reused typescript, with local letter errors','E','These are standard complex-analysis consequences of scalar Cauchy/maximum-principle arguments. The exact Bourbaki draft edition is unverified; the inequalities are independently re-proved here with corrected containment explicitly labeled.',['BourbakiFunctions'],comp='proof_audit_root.md §6; root_math.json analytic_estimates.')
C(29,[29],'amplitude additive','Variation of argument along a path avoiding a point is unchanged under endpoint-fixed homotopy and additive under concatenation.','reused proof fragment','E','The statement follows from path lifting to the universal cover of C×. Exact draft provenance was not recovered from the title/document searches; the result itself is independently checked.',['BourbakiFunctions'],comp='proof_audit_root.md §6 gives the covering-space proof.')
C(30,[30],'BTOᵒᵖ→C équivalence ?','Ordinary BT groups correspond to the displayed ordinary filtered F-crystal data over a smooth formal Witt lift.','open on the pages','B','deJong1995 Th4.1.1 gives crystalline Dieudonné equivalence in the finite-p-basis setting and §4.3 computes ordinary extensions. With the actual crystal/connection and divisibility hypotheses this realizes the programme; arbitrary listed matrices without these hypotheses are not included.',['deJong1995','deJong1998Erratum','Katz1981'])
C(31,[31,32],'Φ′=Φ+pL−σL; ω′=ω−dL','The ordinary extension equations and gauge transformations form the indicated two-step complex, with D₁D₀=0.','computed on the pages','B','Exact modern match: deJong1995 Lem4.3.2 and equations4.3.2.1,4.3.4, Lem4.3.5, under the p-basis setup. The sign convention matches by taking the paper’s change parameter b=−L. Katz1981 §4.3 gives the ordinary logarithmic coordinate connection.',['deJong1995','Katz1981'],comp='root_math.json ordinary_matrix_gauge and gauge_differentials; universal cancellation in proof audit §7.')
C(32,[33],'ΦA^σ=dΦ+AΦ; ΨΦ=p^i','Matrix horizontality, zero curvature, and inverse up to p^i for finite free p-torsion-free coefficients; isolated arithmetic equals i+2p.','computed on the pages','E','These are the standard connection and semilinear change-of-basis identities used in deJong1995 §2. The curvature bracket notation needs a convention; p-torsion-freeness is needed to descend an equality proved after inverting p.',['deJong1995'],comp='root_math.json isolated_arithmetic; proof audit §7 derives both inverse composites under the explicit assumptions.')
C(33,[34],'φ′/φ=u/u^σ; ω′−ω=−du/u','Rank-one Frobenius/connection gauge equations.','computed on the pages; final complex inconsistent in sign/order','B','The preceding equations give the compatible coboundary(u/σu,−dlogu). The last positive-dlog expression does not give a complex with those conventions. The result is standard rank-one F-connection algebra; the literal text goes to scan review.',['deJong1995'],comp='root_math.json gauge_differentials tests corrected and wrong signs; proof audit §7 gives the general identity.')
C(34,[35],'conn.; naturel; normal','The unnamed overlap/cartesian conditions of scan30.','diagrams without a defined category','F','Searches in the typed-manifold, crystal and archive-reference families did not identify a precise mathematical assertion. No conjecture about crystals or Newton polygons is inferred.',['BourbakiVarieties'],confidence='low')
C(35,[36],'bonne réduction virtuelle d’échelon n','Virtual good reduction together with potentially virtual good reduction of length≤n implies length≤n already over the base trait.','open on the pages for ℓ=p; asserted for ℓ≠p','C','Exactly published as SGA7 IX Remark5.13.1, alongside Definition5.12 and Proposition5.13. The published conjecture is distinguished from the abelian semistable criterion. Exact later filtration-length descent is separately searched in the search ledger; generic semistable comparison alone is not accepted as its solution.',['SGA7IX','Tsuji1998'],comp='For ℓ≠p, a finite quotient acting unipotently in characteristic0 is trivial; filtration-length descent follows from the inertia-unipotence filtration. See proof audit §8.',confidence='medium')
C(36,[37],'rang; dim G=Σr_ν','Rank-one A-linear Dieudonné modules decompose into f components, with dimension Σr_ν and rank_W=ef.','proof sketched and computed','B','Classical perfect-field Dieudonné theory (Oda1969 §3) makes the module calculation legitimate. The special dimension-one formal A-modules are the Lubin–Tate objects of Lubin–Tate1965. The bare printed rank sum must include the ramification factor when measured over W.',['Oda1969','LubinTate1965'],comp='root_math.json integral_Hom_lattices; proof audit §9 computes lengths, height and slope1/(ef).')
C(37,[38],'ϖ=ϖ′; Hom=A ou πA','Nonzero A-linear morphisms of the rank-one isocrystals require equal ϖ; integral morphisms satisfy the cumulative valuation bound.','proved by scalar recurrence on the pages','B','This is a direct rank-one Dieudonné-module calculation within the classical theory. Hua2026 §35 lists the archive assertion as a reconstruction not Lean-verified; it is not used as a certificate. Our recurrence proof and finite checks are separate from that report.',['Oda1969','Hua2026'],comp='root_math.json integral_Hom_lattices; proof audit §9 proves necessity, sufficiency and the A/πA cases.')
C(38,[39],'effectif','Commuting integral ϖ and p^fϖ^{-1} yield an F,V-stable lattice in the A-linear isocrystal.','proof sketched','B','The effectivity criterion is independently proved by a common-lattice construction in the classical Dieudonné setting. This proves the linear-algebra assertion, not a claim about every unspecified base in the draft.',['Oda1969'],comp='proof_audit_root.md §9: finite algebra and bounded F,V orbit argument; no finite numerical test is substituted for this proof.')
C(39,[40],'Ω/pΩ','The Hodge cotangent line equals Ω/pΩ for arbitrary ramification.','false as a literal length equality when e>1; scan reading unresolved','F','The quotient has length e, whereas a one-dimensional cotangent space has length1. The suggested Ω/πΩ is a mathematical candidate only. Classical Dieudonné dimension formulas identify the contradiction but cannot decide the glyph or missing cancellation.',['Oda1969'],comp='root_math.json cotangent_lengths; e=2 is already a countermodel to the displayed equality.',confidence='high mathematical contradiction; low reading confidence')
C(40,[43],'unique à isom. non unique près','Existence and uniqueness of the specified Lubin–Tate lift as an isomorphism class, with nontrivial automorphisms.','PD proof sketched','E','Lubin–Tate1965 Lem1 and Th1 construct the formal O_K module and normalized isomorphisms. This supplies the classical lift after specifying its tangent character; it does not justify the page’s PD proof outside its nilpotence range.',['LubinTate1965','Messing1972'])
C(41,[44],'πA muni de puissances divisées','The idealπA is stable under rational divided powers iff e≤p−1; local nilpotence can fail at equality.','hypothesis used without boundary analysis','B','The valuation criterion is an independent check. Messing’s deformation theorem needs locally nilpotent divided powers, so the boundary e=p−1 cannot be passed merely by proving stability. This distinguishes two hypotheses often conflated in the earlier discussion.',['Messing1972','Illusie1985'],comp='root_math.json PD_valuation_tests; proof audit §10 proves v_π(π^n/n!)=n−e v_p(n!) and the p-power boundary.')
C(42,[45],'α=0; D*(G)=M','The precise two integral A-lattices attached to the lift agree, and α=0.','open on the pages','C','Fontaine–Laffaille1982 and Breuil2000 give integral classifications in their stated ranges; they do not by themselves identify these two insufficiently defined lattice normalizations. The rational uniqueness is much weaker. Exact equality remains a reconstruction task, not a documented general open theorem.',['FontaineLaffaille1982','Breuil2000','Messing1972'],comp='root_math.json dual_lattice_exponents checks the printed exponent inconsistency.',confidence='medium')
C(43,[45],'isogénie; filtration','An arbitrary filtered isocrystal with the indicated rational structure comes from a lift.','not asserted as a proved existence theorem; page explicitly reserves existence','C','Colmez–Fontaine2000 ThA proves admissibility equals weak admissibility in the filtered(φ,N) setting. The line with φ=1 and Fil¹=D has t_H=1,t_N=0 and shows why arbitrary filtrations cannot be inserted. Breuil2000 treats BT realizations with further conditions.',['ColmezFontaine2000','Breuil2000'],comp='root_math.json weak_admissibility; proof audit §11.')
C(44,[46],'générateurs; Gal(Kπ/K)=A*','The Lubin–Tate torsion generators form an A×-torsor and the Tate module has A-rank1.','stated and used','E','Lubin–Tate1965 Th2 and reciprocity calculation establish the statement with the chosen uniformizer and reciprocity convention. The torsor has no canonical generator.',['LubinTate1965'],comp='Rank_A T=1 gives dim_C(T⊗_A C)=1; rank_Zp T=ef gives dim_C(T⊗_Zp C)=ef.')
C(45,[47],'C^σ; poids','The embedding decomposition of the Lubin–Tate Tate module has weight1 at the structural embedding and0 at the others.','deduced using Tate’s theorem','B','Tate1967 Th3 and its uniqueness corollary, together with the formal A-action, give the decomposition. The formulas require semilinearity; under a nonnormal coefficient field the embedding factors must also be permuted appropriately.',['Tate1967','LubinTate1965'],comp='proof audit §12 tracks A-action and the C scalar action; tensor-rank discrepancy in root_math.json.')
C(46,[48],'uniques mod A*','The displayed period eigen-equations determine each nonzero period modulo A×.','false as stated unless a separate integral normalization is imposed','B','Tate1967 Th1 gives C^{G_K}=K. Therefore two nonzero solutions differ by K×. Multiplication by a uniformizer is a direct counterexample to A× uniqueness. The corrected statement is K× uniqueness, or A× after fixing a valuation normalization.',['Tate1967'],comp='root_math.json period_cocycle; proof audit §12 gives the ratio argument.')
C(47,[48],'ni pour J⊂Inertie','There is no untwisted identity-embedding eigenperiod even for every subgroup J of inertia.','false for the literal arbitrary-subgroup quantifier; valid open-subgroup version is intended only as reconstruction','B','J={1} is a counterexample. For an open inertia subgroup the product of embedding characters and the norm/cyclotomic relation reduce nonexistence to Tate’s nonzero-weight invariant theorem. The norm relation is used on inertia, not globally without an unramified factor.',['Tate1967','BettsStix2022'],comp='root_math.json period_cocycle; proof audit §12 separates G_K, inertia and arbitrary subgroups.')
C(48,[41],'contact; composition; troncature','Jet contact is chart invariant; composition is associative; a k≥1 jet with invertible linear term is invertible; truncations are surjective; the top symbol is Sym^kT*⊗E; the stated section/base-change operations follow in the differentiable characteristic-zero setting.','proved or sketched in reused typescript','E','These are standard jet constructions. The supplied typed fragments give direct proofs by coordinates, rechecked in proof audit §13. Bourbaki’s published fascicule and archive draft catalogue verify the family, but no pagewise identity with draft382 is claimed.',['BourbakiVarieties','BourbakiDraftCatalogue'],comp='Exact monomial count and action type in root_math.json; polynomial composition/coordinate proofs in proof audit §13.')
C(49,[41],'dim L^k_{p,q}=q binom(p+k,p)','The space of origin-preserving polynomial k-jets has the printed dimension q binom(p+k,p).','false for the literal formula; missing constant-term subtraction is a reading alarm','E','Independent monomial counting gives q(binom(p+k,p)−1). For p=q=k=1 the printed value2 contradicts the single slope coordinate. This may be a dropped subscript/condition/term in the draft; the scan decides.',['BourbakiVarieties'],comp='root_math.json jet_dimensions checks60(p,q,k)cases, including k=0.')
C(50,[42],'multilinéaire','O-multilinear operations on local sections reconstruct unique multilinear fibre-bundle maps.','proved in the reused text; symmetric/alternating cases left as analogues','E','Local frames make the construction explicit, and uniqueness gives compatibility on overlaps. This is standard bundle algebra; no exact identification of the numbered draft edition is claimed.',['BourbakiVarieties'],comp='proof audit §13: coefficient functions on local frames glue; no characteristic-p polarization argument is used.')
C(51,[49],'orbite; transverse','The stated regularity, homogeneous-space and transverse-fiber-product properties under their displayed differentiability/local-closedness/countability hypotheses.','reused proof fragments','E','The implicit-function and constant-rank arguments recheck the local statements. The complete-metric sentence depends on the absent global manifold conventions and is carried only conditionally. No new crystalline theorem is extracted.',['BourbakiVarieties'],comp='proof audit §13 gives coordinate transversality/constant-rank checks and the exact global qualification.')
C(52,[50],'rational comparisons','The displayed legible comparisons of rational numbers are arithmetically true.','computed without an identified geometric proposition','F','Several literal alternatives are false or undefined, and others are correct. There is no identified geometric theorem whose fate can be assigned from these unlabelled figures. Searches in the polygon/Dieudonné families found no exact identification.',['Oda1969'],comp='root_math.json polygon_arithmetic gives every tested comparison and exact signed difference, retaining alternatives.',confidence='high arithmetic; low interpretation')
C(53,[51],'pro-réductif','A rigid semisimple Q_p-linear neutral Tannakian category has proreductive tensor automorphism group.','conditional assertion; fibre functor assumed','B','Deligne–Milne1982 Th2.11 and Prop2.23 give reconstruction and the characteristic-zero semisimplicity criterion. Connectedness does not follow. The theorem does not construct the manuscript’s hypothetical arithmetic fibre functor.',['DeligneMilne1982'])
C(54,[51],'réseau stable','The proposed class of effective filtered F-objects forms a rigid category admitting the intended T_p.','open; definition incomplete','C','Weak admissibility is essential for rational realizations (Colmez–Fontaine ThA). Effectivity alone is not dual-stable: F=p on a line has a stable lattice, but its dual F=p^{-1} has none. The category must be enlarged or restricted explicitly.',['ColmezFontaine2000'],comp='root_math.json weak_admissibility; proof audit §11 includes the valuation proof for the dual lattice.')
C(55,[52],'T_p pleinement fidèle ⇒ G enveloppe','Full faithfulness alone identifies the Tannakian group with the algebraic envelope of the Galois image.','false as a purely categorical implication','B','Deligne–Milne Prop2.21 requires stability under subobjects. Restriction Rep(SL₂)→Rep(B) is exact and fully faithful in characteristic0, but the image B is proper; the standard B-module has a line absent as an SL₂-subobject. This is a counterexample to the claimed formal inference, not to a named arithmetic realization.',['DeligneMilne1982'],comp='root_math.json Borel_restriction_invariants; proof audit §14 proves full faithfulness for all representations, beyond finite tests.')
C(56,[52],'i₁ i₂=i central','Two commuting weight cocharacters with central product determine the proposed bigrading and an isomorphism torsor between fibre functors.','construction sketched','B','The torsor formalism is Deligne–Milne1982 Th3.2; a central product makes one cocharacter the central cocharacter times the inverse of the other. The unspecified field of the Hodge fibre functor remains a reading/definition issue.',['DeligneMilne1982'],comp='Group calculation: i₂=i·i₁^{-1}; centrality gives commutation. No canonical torsor point follows.')
C(57,[53],'f_{uρ}=i₁(u^{-1})','Rescaling the period changes the comparison point by the Hodge cocharacter.','computed but printed equation omits the torsor factor','B','The well-typed reconstruction is f_{uρ}=f_ρ∘i₁(u^{-1}) (or the corresponding left action). It follows directly by applying the scaling to each graded piece. The printed group element alone cannot be a point of a nontrivial isomorphism torsor.',['DeligneMilne1982'],comp='root_math.json period_cocycle; proof audit §15 includes the rank-one cocycle and the action convention.')
C(58,[54],'recollement unipotent','Splittings of the filtered fibre functor with fixed associated-graded comparison form a unipotent torsor.','construction sketched','B','Ziegler’s arXiv1111.1981 Th4.13 gives fpqc local splitting and Th4.14 identifies the splitting torsor; Th4.15 gives global splitting over an affine base under pro-smoothness. Numbering differs from the published edition. The additional crystal/Galois compatibilities are not automatic.',['Ziegler2015'])
C(59,[54],'Frobenius; Q^σ','Frobenius is σ-linear on the crystalline realization and becomes an element of the tensor group after a trivialization.','construction sketched','B','Changing trivialization sends b to g^{-1}bσ(g). Rational comparison theory keeps the unramified crystalline coefficient field distinct from the generally ramified de Rham field; Tsuji1998 §2.1–2.2 spells out that distinction.',['Tsuji1998'],comp='proof audit §15 derives σ-conjugation; scan67 DR/cr ambiguity remains unresolved.')
C(60,[55],'F_∞²=1','The real comparison transports Betti conjugation to a real de Rham involution.','construction sketched','B','For a comparison c compatible with conjugation, F∞=c·c_B·c^{-1} is real and squares to1. This is a conditional linear-algebra consequence, not existence of all proposed realizations.',['DeligneMilne1982'],comp='proof audit §15: bar(c)=c c_B and c_B²=1 imply bar(F∞)=F∞ and F∞²=1.')
C(61,[55],'f_p — —','Existence of all proposed tensor-compatible Betti, de Rham, étale and crystalline comparisons.','open; a p-adic comparison is literally left blank','C','Tsuji1998 Th2.2.1 proves the rational semistable comparison in its proper semistable setting; BMS2018 supplies integral comparisons for proper smooth formal schemes over O_C. These are precise realizations of parts of the programme, not a theorem for an undefined category of all motives.',['Tsuji1998','BhattMorrowScholze2018'],confidence='medium')
C(62,[56],'Hodge simple, générale','Classical rational and generalized Hodge-conjecture agenda.','open on the pages; names only','C','The classical rational conjecture remains unsolved as recorded by the current Clay problem page. Grothendieck1969 explains why the naive general formulation needs correction. These named conjectures are reconstructed separately; no unstated quantifiers are attributed to scan75.',['ClayHodge2026','Grothendieck1969Hodge'],confidence='high named conjectures; medium identification')
C(63,[56],'Tate simple, générale','Tate cycle-conjecture agenda for smooth projective varieties over finitely generated fields.','open on the pages; field/codimension unspecified','C','Tate1966 proves the homomorphism theorem for abelian varieties over finite fields and the divisor consequences. General cycle assertions remain broader; the recent Li2026 preprint is explicitly conditional on dimension≤4 and is not a general solution. No local-field full-faithfulness claim is inferred.',['Tate1966','Li2026'],confidence='medium')
C(64,[57],'Frobenius et connexion; filtration','Compatibility of de Rham filtration and connection with Frobenius.','agenda without exact quantifiers','C','Crystalline comparison provides a horizontal Frobenius and a separate Hodge filtration in precise geometric settings. Weak admissibility does not require Frobenius to preserve that filtration: the rank-two example in the proof audit is weakly admissible but not filtration-stable.',['ColmezFontaine2000','Tsuji1998'],comp='root_math.json weak_admissibility: diag(1,p), Fil¹=span(e₀+e₁).')
C(65,[57],'double réseau ?; groupe de Tate','The double-lattice construction and Tate group over the suggested bases.','open; definitions not supplied','F','BMS2018 Th4.28 identifies finite free Breuil–Kisin–Fargues modules with pairs(T,Ξ), but there is no evidence that scan75 names this exact object. The global Tate-group question has no defined source category/fibre functor. They remain identifiable research leads only after reconstruction.',['BhattMorrowScholze2018','DeligneMilne1982'],confidence='low')
C(66,[58],'changement de base lisse','Smooth base change for torsion étale coefficients prime to residual characteristics.','reused proof fragments','A','SGA4 XVI Cor1.2 is the exact published smooth-base-change statement; the draft’s low-degree argument and higher-degree induction belong to this exposition. The H^q/R¹ mismatch on68 is not normalized.',['SGA4XVI'])
C(67,[58],'classe fondamentale; transitivité','Divisor Kummer class, purity for smooth pairs, independence and transitivity of fundamental classes.','proof sketched in reused fragments; last proof cut off','A','SGA4 XVI §3, especially Th3.7, Cor3.8 and Rem3.10, gives the relative smooth-pair framework. The invariant formula for untwisted input is Ri!Z/n≃Z/n(−c)[−2c]; a trivialization of the twist is additional. SGA4 XIX Th3.4 has its historical resolution hypothesis.',['SGA4XVI','SGA4XIX'],comp='proof audit §16 derives the twist from the Kummer boundary and checks codimension-additivity.')
C(68,[59],'translations; convolution','Weighted translation and convolution bounds and strong L^p continuity, with the stated restricted p=∞ endpoint.','proved in reused typescript','E','These are Bourbaki Integration VIII results. The endpoint restriction is correct and essential; the exact weighted dual identity needs the preceding Haar convention. The draft number357 is a provenance link, not a claim that all modern edition numbering agrees.',['BourbakiIntegration','BourbakiDraftCatalogue'],comp='root_math.json analytic_estimates gives the all-L∞ counterexample; proof audit §17 derives Minkowski bounds and a convention-explicit unimodular check.')
C(69,[60],'ℓ_G ≃ M⊗^L_Z O_S','For a group of multiplicative type with character sheaf M, the co-Lie complex is M⊗^L_Z O_S.','proof sketched, continued on scan79','B','Exact later statement: Bergh–Rydh2019 PropB.4, whose proof uses the augmentation of the group algebra and a locally free resolution. This matches the object and derived tensor, including descent, rather than only the general subject of cotangent complexes.',['BerghRydh2019'],comp='root_math.json mu_n_coLie; proof audit §18 gives μ_n and a free-character resolution.')
C(70,[60],'⊗^L_Λ; ⊗^L_Z','Derived coefficient-change associativity for the co-Lie complex.','computed and continued on cote8:79','E','Associativity of derived tensor gives the stated base change once the module structures and flat/free resolutions are fixed. It is not ordinary tensor in the presence of torsion. The continuation was read in both packages.',['BerghRydh2019'],comp='[O --n→O] computes the derived tensor of Z/n; multiplication by n invertible gives0, and n=p over F_p gives two nonzero cohomology groups.')

Pm(5,'A derived crystalline object for finite flat groups',[15,16],'Construct the proposed perfect complex, with both cohomology modules and the co-Lie triangles.','Specify the relative crystalline base and derived functor; compare it with the cotangent-complex construction before asserting an arbitrary-base equivalence.','deJong1993 Introduction/§3 treats a relative construction; Illusie1985 §2 gives the co-Lie framework. The canceled variant is retained only as historical evidence.','The page cancels one variant; no source located explains the later abandonment of this exact presentation.','Worth one focused reconstruction run to make the triangles into a genuinely defined functor.',['deJong1993','Illusie1985'])
Pm(6,'Triangle compatibilities and two cross-composites',[17,18],'Prove the flagged α,β compatibility and decide the two degree-two composites marked nul?.','Fix the two triangles, signs and Frobenius twists, then calculate their Yoneda products; no automatic triangulated-category vanishing is assumed.','Modern deformation/co-Lie complexes in Illusie1985 §§2–3 provide a comparison target, but no exact theorem for these unnamed composites was found.','The source itself contains the inequality/equality conflict and a deferred similar proof; later reasons are undocumented.','Worth resolving after the scan clarifies the conflicting compatibility sign.',['Illusie1985'])
Pm(7,'Stack, faithfulness and reconstruction for enriched objects',[20,21],'Do the objects form a stack; are the forgetful/enriched functors faithful or fully faithful; does the triple recover the group, including F=0?','Use a precisely defined target; height-one groups, finite flat groups and BT groups are separate domains.','deJong1993 §2 settles height-one cases; deJong–Messing1999 Prop1.2 and Th4.6 give qualified faithfulness; Lau Th1.3 and ALB Th4.74 classify BT groups using additional structure.','The manuscript leaves morphisms and coherence unspecified. Lau explains that divided Frobenius contains information lost in the presence of PD torsion.','Worth comparing the proposed extra triangles with modern divided structure, without claiming the categories already agree.',['deJong1993','deJongMessing1999','Lau2018_2026','AnschutzLeBras2023'])
Pm(8,'Recovering the Hodge submodule by Frobenius descent',[21],'The displayed equality of pullbacks determines ω uniquely; then recover the remaining structure.','Under BT1 exactness, prove the necessary faithfulness/descent statement for the specified crystalline pullback, or add a flatness/perfectness hypothesis.','Oda1969 Cor5.11 is an exact positive perfect-field abelian case; Illusie1985 separates BT1 from arbitrary groups killed by p.','The printed proof passes from equality of pullbacks to equality without a descent justification.','Worth a short counterexample-or-descent audit after identifying the exact pullback functor.',['Oda1969','Illusie1985'])
Pm(9,'PD lifts and relative obstruction groups',[19,22,23],'Explain the filtration attached to a lift, relative Ext⁰=0, the degree-two obstruction and the suggested nonzero-kernel example.','Define the homotopy-fibre deformation complex and compare its degree0/1/2 groups; for BT groups impose locally nilpotent PD data.','Messing1972 V.1.6 and Illusie1985 Prop3.2/§4 provide exact deformation statements. No realization of the scan22 linear kernel was found.','The page ends with sans doute and does not specify the relative Ext category.','Worth a finite derived-category reconstruction; claiming a finite-flat counterexample requires an actual realization.',['Messing1972','Illusie1985'])
Pm(10,'Ordinary BT classification and extension equations',[30,31,32],'Is D:BTOᵒᵖ→C an equivalence with the six listed data?','Use the smooth finite-p-basis base and actual filtered Dieudonné crystal conditions; match the ordinary extension gauge class.','deJong1995 Th4.1.1 and Lem4.3.2 supply classification and the exact extension equations; Katz1981 §4.3 supplies ordinary logarithmic coordinates.','No source states why the specific draft was left unfinished; later work defines a more precise target.','Worth a concise annotated comparison; its core mathematical classification is established.',['deJong1995','deJong1998Erratum','Katz1981'])
Pm(11,'Descent of the length of virtual good reduction',[36],'For a BT pro-ℓ group with virtual good reduction and potential length≤n, obtain length≤n over the original henselian trait.','Retain the exact trait, strict subgroup, integral length and ℓ=p distinction of SGA7 IX5.12–5.13.1.','The question is published as SGA7 IX Remark5.13.1. The ℓ≠p case follows from inertia unipotence. Exact later p-case fate is treated in the dedicated search supplement; a semistable comparison theorem alone is insufficient.','The published source calls the p-case plausible and gives no reason it was left aside.','Worth an exact literature/proof comparison of filtration descent rather than another general monodromy survey.',['SGA7IX','Tsuji1998'])
Pm(12,'Effectivity of the A-linear isocrystal',[37,38,39],'Find integral F,V-stable lattices and identify A-linear isogenies and Hom lattices.','Use rank-one A-components and the explicit common-integrality criterion forϖ,p^fϖ^{-1}.','Classical Dieudonné theory legitimizes the modules; the recurrence and common-lattice argument are independently checked here. Hua2026 explicitly does not provide a formal certificate for the archive isogeny assertion.','No historical explanation of nonpublication was located.','A short write-up could turn the checked scalar argument into a clean companion lemma.',['Oda1969','Hua2026'])
Pm(13,'The PD proof of the Lubin–Tate lift',[43,44],'Lift G₀ with prescribed A-action uniquely up to nonunique isomorphism by lifting the Hodge filtration.','Distinguish PD stability from local nilpotence; compare the actual LT construction when e=p−1 or larger.','Lubin–Tate1965 constructs the group; Messing1972 V.1.6 controls nilpotent-PD lifts. The independent valuation calculation isolates the equality boundary.','The page invokes a PD ideal without checking this boundary; no later historical explanation was found.','Worth a tightly scoped boundary-case comparison, not a new LT existence project.',['LubinTate1965','Messing1972'])
Pm(14,'The two integral lattices and the exponent α',[40,45],'Prove D*(G)=M and α=0, with the tangent/cotangent identifications.','Define both lattices and their variance and normalizations; then prove equality in a specified ramification range or compute the discrepancy.','Fontaine–Laffaille1982 and Breuil2000 classify suitable integral objects but do not identify these particular definitions from the fragment. The literal quotient/sign discrepancies are unresolved readings.','The page explicitly reserves this proof; no source explaining its fate was found.','High value after scan review: an explicit rank-one comparison may settle the exact question in one run.',['FontaineLaffaille1982','Breuil2000','Messing1972'])
Pm(15,'Lubin–Tate period equations and normalization',[46,47,48],'Find the embedding periods, their uniqueness and the obstruction to an untwisted identity period.','Use semilinear Galois actions, K× uniqueness, the chosen Tate twist and open-inertia quantifiers; add integral normalization before A× uniqueness.','Tate1967 Th1–3 and LT reciprocity give the precise corrected statements; the norm/cyclotomic identity is used on inertia.','The page’s modA× and arbitraryJ wording overreach without extra data; no historical abandonment claim is made.','Worth a compact normalized calculation once the tensor bases and signs are checked on the scans.',['Tate1967','LubinTate1965','BettsStix2022'])
Pm(16,'An actual arithmetic fibre functor',[51,52],'Construct a suitable T_p or Δ, prove full faithfulness and identify the group envelope.','Choose an admissible tensor category and prove closure under subobjects in the realization; full faithfulness alone does not suffice.','Deligne–Milne Prop2.21 gives the correct criterion; Colmez–Fontaine ThA gives the admissible filtered-module equivalence in its rational setting.','The source assumes suitability rather than defining it; the Borel counterexample isolates the missing categorical hypothesis.','Worth reconstructing the intended category; the abstract group theorem itself needs no new research.',['DeligneMilne1982','ColmezFontaine2000'])
Pm(17,'Splitting and period comparison torsors',[53,54],'Relate graded, filtered, de Rham and crystalline fibre functors by unipotent torsors and period choices.','Fix the graded comparison, cocharacter convention, coefficient fields and σ-conjugacy; then impose the desired arithmetic compatibility.','Ziegler Th4.13–4.15 establishes the filtered splitting torsor; the period scaling/cocycle calculation is checked independently.','The page has an omitted torsor factor and DR/cr ambiguity; no source gives a historical explanation.','Worth matching the diagrams to a defined example before formulating a global conjecture.',['Ziegler2015','DeligneMilne1982','Tsuji1998'])
Pm(18,'The realization system and the blank p-adic comparison',[55],'Construct compatible Betti, de Rham, ℓ-adic and crystalline realizations, Frobenii and real involution.','Formulate a precise geometric category and use period rings for p-adic comparison, rather than filling the blank map by analogy.','Tsuji1998 Th2.2.1 proves rational semistable comparison; BMS2018 proves integral comparisons for proper smooth formal schemes overO_C. General motivic compatibility remains a different scope.','The map on73 is unfinished and the page itself says non? beside a revised diagram.','Worth an annotated historical-to-modern diagram, with the general motive problem kept separate.',['Tsuji1998','BhattMorrowScholze2018'])
Pm(19,'Hodge conjectures named in the agenda',[56],'Hodge simple, générale.','Separate rational Hodge classes on smooth projective complex varieties from Grothendieck’s corrected generalized coniveau formulation.','The current Clay page records the classical problem as unsolved; Grothendieck1969 corrects the naive generalized version.','The sheet gives no reasons or exact formulations; the difficulty cannot be historically diagnosed from these words.','Important background, but a few-hour run should identify intended scope rather than attempt the general conjecture.',['ClayHodge2026','Grothendieck1969Hodge'])
Pm(20,'Tate conjectures named in the agenda',[56],'Tate simple, générale.','Separate homomorphisms/divisors from higher-codimension cycle classes and specify the finitely generated ground field.','Tate1966 establishes the finite-field abelian homomorphism theorem and divisor consequences; Li2026’s recent higher-dimensional result is conditional and does not close the general conjecture.','The sheet contains only names; no specific reason for leaving them open is documented.','Worth identifying which implication the surrounding fibre-functor discussion actually needs.',['Tate1966','Li2026'])
Pm(21,'Frobenius, filtration and the double lattice',[57],'Frobenius/connection compatibility and double réseau?, revoir.','Keep a horizontal Frobenius separate from Hodge-filtration stability; specify the two integral lattices before comparing them.','Colmez–Fontaine ThA controls rational admissibility; BMS2018 Th4.28 supplies a distinct modern pair(T,Ξ) framework. Neither silently identifies the notation on75.','The question is marked for reconsideration; no reason for abandonment appears.','Worth a small reconstruction with an ordinary or LT example, followed by an exact lattice comparison.',['ColmezFontaine2000','BhattMorrowScholze2018'])
Pm(22,'The Tate group over several possible bases',[57],'Define the groupe de Tate over a finite-type Z-base, a field or a mixed-characteristic local base.','Specify a neutral or nonneutral tensor category of realizations and its fibre functors before naming its automorphism group.','Deligne–Milne1982 gives the abstract group/gerbe formalism, but no exact universal group is identifiable in this fragment.','The alternatives are listed without definitions.','Low short-run payoff until the surrounding category is reconstructed.',['DeligneMilne1982'])
Pm(23,'Deferred standard steps in the reused typescripts',[25,28,29,41,42,49,58,59],'Supply the flagged effacement argument, analogous jet/multilinear assertions, purity interpolation and the convolution density step.','Use each result’s actual torsion, differentiability, invertibility, Haar and endpoint hypotheses; retain missing neighboring pages as missing.','SGA4 XII/XVI give the published étale results. Coordinate and analytic arguments are rechecked in the proof audit. Exact Bourbaki draft numbering remains a provenance limitation.','The sources explicitly request a fuller argument or leave analogues to the reader; no claim of a new open crystalline problem is justified.','Useful editorial work for a companion, with low research novelty.',['SGA4XII','SGA4XVI','BourbakiVarieties','BourbakiIntegration'])
Pm(24,'Multiplicative-type co-Lie formula and descent',[60],'Identify ℓ_G with M⊗^L_Z O_S and perform the coefficient change continued on79.','Use a locally free resolution of the character sheaf, derived tensor and étale descent.','Bergh–Rydh2019 PropB.4 is an exact later theorem; the μ_n calculation here tests degrees and torsion explicitly.','The fragment supplies a sketch, not a documented historically unresolved obstruction.','Worth a short companion proof; the mathematical result is established.',['BerghRydh2019'])

